Exercise 8.11 defines the neighbourhood system \(R=\{[0,r)\mid r\in\mathbb{Q},\ 0<r\le 1\}\) and the relation \([0,r)\ a\ [0,s)\) if and only if \(r<s\) or \(r=s=1\), and asks to show that this is an approximable map \(a:R\to R\), that \(a\) is a projection, that the fixed-point set of \(a\) is in one-one correspondence with the real numbers in \([0,1]\) with inclusion matching the usual order, and that \(a\) is not finitary because, aside from \(\bot\), that fixed-point set has no finite elements. `R` is that family, with master \([0,1)\), and `R_mem_iff` is the membership criterion; nestedness of the intervals makes the intersection of two neighbourhoods the neighbourhood at the minimum endpoint (`Ico0_inter`). `aRel` is Scott's relation on endpoints in \((0,1]\), `aRel_iff` is the displayed biconditional, and `a` is the approximable map: related sets are \(R\)-neighbourhoods, the master is related to itself by the clause \(r=s=1\), `aRel_combine` closes outputs under intersection, and monotonicity compares endpoints of nested intervals. Definition 8.3 makes a projection a retraction lying under the identity. `isRetraction_a` is \(a\circ a=a\): two strict steps compose, a strict step followed by the diagonal stays strict, a diagonal step followed by a strict step is impossible, and two diagonal steps stay on \([0,1)\), while a strict pair is split by a rational strictly between the endpoints. `isProjection_a` adds \(a\le\mathrm{id}\) because the relation forces \(r\le s\), so \([0,r)\subseteq[0,s)\). `example_811` is the conjunction of that projection, the retraction, and the failure of finitariness. The correspondence is the order isomorphism `fixOrderIso`. The cut set of an element is the set of radii whose intervals belong to it, the cut point is one minus the infimum of that set (`cutPoint`), and `cutPoint_nonneg` with `cutPoint_le_one` place it in \([0,1]\); the orientation \(1-\inf\) makes larger filters correspond to larger reals. `cutU` is the up-set of radii \(r\le 1\) with \(1-t<r\) or \(r=1\), `cutElt` is the element of that up-set, and `toElementMap_cutElt` shows it is fixed: membership of \([0,s)\) after applying \(a\) is a strict step from an earlier cut witness or the diagonal at \(1\), and density of the rationals supplies the strict witness in the other direction. `cutElt_cutPoint` shows every fixed point equals the cut element of its cut point, `cutElt_injective` and `cutElt_le_iff` give the inverse and the equivalence of inclusion of cut elements with \(\le\) on \([0,1]\). `not_isFinitary_a` uses Definition 8.3's reading of finitary, that the fixed-point set would be order-isomorphic to the elements of some neighbourhood system. Transporting `fixOrderIso` along that isomorphism, `not_isCompactElt_pos` shows that the image of each \(t\) with \(0<t\le 1\) fails to be compact, because it is the directed supremum of the images of \([0,t)\) and lies above none of them; every principal approximant of the image of \(1\) is therefore the image of \(0\), the algebraic supremum of those approximants collapses the image of \(1\) to the image of \(0\), and injectivity yields \(1=0\). **Theorem:** yes — `a` is the displayed approximable map, `isProjection_a` is the projection, `fixOrderIso` is the order-isomorphism of the fixed points with \([0,1]\), and `not_isFinitary_a` is the failure of finitariness. **Proof:** yes — idempotence is the endpoint case split, the inequality under the identity is \(r\le s\), the Dedekind up-sets implement the hint that inclusion matches \(\le\), and the collapse of the top to the bottom is the hint that \(\bot\) is the only finite fixed point.
